  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 333 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nAs the weekend approaches, attention turns to the approach of the \nremnants of Hurricane Isaias, which is forecast to make \nlandfall along the Gulf Coast late tomorrow, and graze southeast\nMissouri and southwest Illinois as an extratropical cyclone \nsometime between late Saturday and early Sunday. Model consensus\nregarding the track of this hurricane and its remnant circulation\nhas improved over the past 24 hours, although at this time range \nthere is still plenty of uncertainty. At some point as this low \napproaches the Bootheel it is likely to turn eastward, and exactly\nhow quickly this occurs will determine who sees rain and how \nmuch. A fast turn to the east would keep most of the area \nrelatively dry, while a slower turn that allows the circulation to\ndrift further north would result in both greater coverage and \nrain amounts. Even in the more bullish solutions, rain amounts \nwould likely be of the \"beneficial\" variety, as 75th-90th \npercentile amounts only just approach 1 inch along our \nsoutheastern border. On the flip-side, 10th-25th percentile \namounts only result in a few hundredths to near 1/10th of an inch,\nso there is still a reasonable chance that most of the area \nmisses out. In any case, increased cloud cover is likely to stunt \nour heating in some areas Saturday, although we will likely remain\nabove average. \n\nAnother \"sneaky\" potential to keep an eye on Saturday is for another \nday of limited elevated fire weather conditions. While we expect \nhumidity to steadily increase, thus may not occur until late \nSaturday, and breezy southeast winds may result in pockets of \nelevated conditions here and there. There are a lot of things that \nwould need to overlap in order to realize this threat, but a slower \narrival of the remnant circulation and its associated cloud \ncover/humidity would make this threat a bit more realistic. By \nsometime late Sunday, whatever rain does manage to make it this \nfar north should come to an end. By Monday, southwesterly low \nlevel flow is expected to resume in earnest, and the ambient upper\nridge will reassert itself. As such, a quick rebound to near-\nrecord temperatures appears likely, with renewed potential for \nelevated fire weather conditions. The coverage of this threat will\nof course be influenced by how much rain falls the day prior, but\nif the drier solutions pan out, there is a higher ceiling for \nmore widespread elevated conditions. \n\nFrom Tuesday onward, day-to-day details become much less \npredictable, as the upper flow pattern appears to gradually shift \neastward and weaken. This will make the area more prone to \nmeaningful cold fronts, and eventually precipitation, but exactly \nhow quickly this unfolds is the key. A high degree of variance \nexists among ensembles, with massive temperature spreads (15-20+ \ndegree interquartile ranges) Tuesday onward due to the presence of \nan oscillating surface front. Meanwhile, another lull in \nprecipitation chances appears likely immediately in the wake of the \npassing tropical remnants TUE/WED, but ensemble precipitation \nchances steadily increase Thursday onward as more substantial \ntroughs/cold fronts begin to encroach on the weakening ridge. \nOverall, while the potential for more warm extremes seems lower \nbeyond Monday, it is a bit early to make many bold proclamations \nregarding temperature/precipitation specifics until we get a better \nhandle on the position of that frontal boundary.",
